# L §5.16 Closed forms: quantile(a * X + b, q) is "a · quantile(D, q) + b for a > 0; a · quantile(D, 1 - q) + b for
# a < 0", correctly rounded to the line's to u. z = normal(0, 1): at 50 % the median 0, detected exactly (L §7.3), so
# 2 · 0 + 1 = 1 exactly; at 97.5 % 2 · 1.95996... + 1 = 4.9199... -> 4.92, and with a = -2, -2 · quantile(D, 2.5 %) + 1
# = -2 · (-1.95996...) + 1 -> 4.92, both resting on a transcendental (≈, L §7.3 The error bound). u01 = uniform(0, 1):
# 3 · 0.25 + 1 = 1.75 and -3 · quantile(D, 75 %) = -2.25, exact. (Writer 8: was REFCORE-NOT-YET, to u on a closed-form
# quantile of an affine form.)
=== m.tally
model t
  tally 2
  on 2026-12-31
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source s "s"
input z: dist
input u01: dist
input two: num
input three: num

version v
  cite s
  governs always
  known 2026-01-01
  z = normal(0, 1) to 1e-9
  u01 = uniform(0, 1) to 0.001
  two = 2
  three = 3

ask a: quantile(two * z + 1, 50 %) to 0.01, quantile(two * z + 1, 97.5 %) to 0.01, quantile(-two * z + 1, 97.5 %) to 0.01
ask b: quantile(three * u01 + 1, 25 %) to 0.01, quantile(-three * u01, 25 %) to 0.01
=== expect
a: (1 (horizon none), ≈ 4.92 ± 0.005 (horizon none), ≈ 4.92 ± 0.005 (horizon none))
  cut 2026-12-31
b: (1.75 (horizon none), -2.25 (horizon none))
  cut 2026-12-31
